    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 312259\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n359 PM PDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Flash Flood Watch in effect for northern Mohave County today, with \n  shower and thunderstorm potential across our eastern zones.\n\n* Relatively drier and cooler conditions expected through the work\n  week, though uncertainty increases this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Sunday.\n\nThe concern of the day will be our eastern zones, with primary focus \nacross northern Mohave County. Today, the region has between 0.75 \nand 1.00 inches of PWAT. Simultaneously, there is longwave troughing \nover the western CONUS that will usher a shortwave into the southern \nGreat Basin this afternoon. Coupled with daytime heating, forecast \nHRRR soundings continue to show over 2500 J/kg of SBCAPE across \nnorthwestern Arizona today. As of 10 AM PDT/MST, we have issued a \nSevere Thunderstorm Warning east of Kingman along Interstate 40 near \nFort Rock (expired 9:45am) and currently have a Flood Advisory in \neffect for areas east of Kingman due to training thunderstorms. \nGauges in the area have picked up around 0.50 inches over the last \nhour. Showers and thunderstorms will increase in coverage as the day \ngoes on and will be efficient rain-makers, resulting in flash \nflooding. Be weather aware today if spending time outdoors or \ndriving on roads with low-water crossings across Mohave County. \nCannot rule out thunderstorms in eastern Clark or eastern Lincoln \ncounties as well, particularly with convective initiation off of \noutflow boundaries. The Flash Flood Watch is in effect for the \nArizona Strip, which includes areas along and north of the Colorado \nRiver in Mohave County until midnight tonight.\n\nThroughout the work week, a dry southwesterly flow continues across \nthe area. Moisture from Hurricane Karina in the eastern Pacific has \nthe potential to get wrapped up into the flow aloft, which will \nbring increased cloud cover to the region, which will assist with \ncooler afternoon temperatures. Looking ahead late-week into the \nweekend, there is considerable uncertainty as it pertains to chances \nof precipitation. There is a tropical disturbance in the \nsoutheastern Pacific Ocean with a 90 percent chance of formation in \nthe next 48 hours. The trajectory of this future storm will highly \nimpact precipitation chances in the Mojave Desert... Should it take \non a more westward / southward trajectory, then we could simply get \nincreased cloud cover. Will continue to assess as we move through \nthe week.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...South-\nsouthwest winds likely to continue throughout the TAF period, \nstrongest from late morning through sunset with gusts 20-30 knots. \nThere remains a low (~25%) chance of northeasterly outflow winds \nreaching the terminal between 03z-08z, but no convection in the \nvicinity. Few to scattered clouds around 12kft, with mostly clear \nskies late tonight and tomorrow. Temps remain below 100F.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Isolated to scattered \nstorms continue in Mohave and Lincoln counties this evening, \nclearing out overnight. Gusty outflow winds, hail, and heavy rain \npossible with the strongest cores. Elsewhere, breezy southerly to \nwesterly winds persist with gusts generally 15-30 knots. Winds \nsubside after sunset, but remain breezy in the KDAG area into the \novernight hours. Few to scattered clouds at or above 10kft, with \nscattered to broken conditions in northern Mohave and eastern \nLincoln counties.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
